.TH "vsearch-uchime3_denovo" "1" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --uchime3_denovo \[em] detect chimeras \f[I]de novo\f[R] using
the UCHIME2 algorithm with a stricter abundance skew
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--uchime3_denovo\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
(\f[B]--borderline\f[R] | \f[B]--chimeras\f[R] | \f[B]--nonchimeras\f[R]
| \f[B]--uchimealns\f[R] | \f[B]--uchimeout\f[R]) \f[I]filename\f[R]
[\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--uchime3_denovo\f[R] detects chimeric
sequences present in the fasta- or fastq-formatted \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
(quality values are ignored), without the use of external references
(\f[I]de novo\f[R]).
It uses the UCHIME2 algorithm, which is designed for denoised amplicons
(see \f[V]--cluster_unoise\f[R]).
The only difference from \f[V]--uchime2_denovo\f[R] is that the default
minimum abundance skew (\f[V]--abskew\f[R]) is set to 16.0 rather than
2.0, reflecting the higher abundance contrast expected between genuine
sequences and chimeras in well-denoised datasets.
Sequences are compared on their \f[I]plus\f[R] strand only.
.PP
Chimera detection is based on a scoring function controlled by two
options: \f[V]--dn\f[R] and \f[V]--xn\f[R].
Note that \f[V]--mindiffs\f[R], \f[V]--mindiv\f[R], and \f[V]--minh\f[R]
are ignored by this command.
.PP
Input sequences should carry abundance annotations in their headers
(e.g.\ \f[V];size=integer;\f[R]).
\f[V]--sizein\f[R] is always implied; it does not need to be specified.
Entries without annotations silently count as \f[V]size=1\f[R]: when no
sequence carries an annotation, no candidate can pass the default
abundance-skew requirement (see \f[V]--abskew\f[R]) and no chimera is
ever reported.
Sequences are automatically sorted by decreasing abundance before
chimera detection, and each sequence is compared only against the
previously processed, more abundant sequences that were classified
non-chimeric (borderline and chimeric sequences are never used as
parents).
The assumption is that chimeras appear later in the PCR amplification
process and are therefore less abundant than their parents (see
\f[V]--abskew\f[R]).
.PP
At least one output option must be specified.
.PP
See also \f[V]--uchime_denovo\f[R] for the original UCHIME algorithm and
\f[V]--uchime_ref\f[R] for reference-based chimera detection.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.TP
\f[V]--uchime3_denovo\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
Detect chimeras \f[I]de novo\f[R] in the fasta- or fastq-formatted
\f[I]fastxfile\f[R] (quality values are ignored) using the UCHIME2
algorithm with a stricter default abundance skew.
This option is mandatory.
.PP
At least one of the following output options must be specified:
.TP
\f[V]--borderline\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write borderline chimeric sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta
format.
Borderline chimeric sequences are sequences with a chimera score high
enough to be suspicious, but not sufficiently different from their
closest parent to be classified as chimeric.
Note: this command never classifies a sequence as borderline (its
scoring is all-or-nothing), so the file is always empty and
\f[V]--uchimeout\f[R] never reports \f[V]?\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--chimeras\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write chimeric sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
Output order may vary when using multiple threads.
.TP
\f[V]--nonchimeras\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write non-chimeric sequences to \f[I]filename\f[R], in fasta format.
Output order may vary when using multiple threads.
.TP
\f[V]--uchimealns\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write three-way global alignments (parentA, parentB, chimera) to
\f[I]filename\f[R] in a human-readable format.
All sequences are converted to upper case before alignment.
Lower case letters indicate disagreement in the alignment.
Output order may vary when using multiple threads.
Use \f[V]--alignwidth\f[R] to modify the alignment width.
.TP
\f[V]--uchimeout\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write chimera detection results to \f[I]filename\f[R] using an 18-field,
tab-separated uchime-like format.
Output row order may vary when using multiple threads.
Use \f[V]--uchimeout5\f[R] for a format compatible with usearch version
5 and earlier.
The 18 fields are:
.RS
.IP " 1." 4
score: higher score means a more likely chimeric alignment.
.IP " 2." 4
Q: query sequence label.
.IP " 3." 4
A: parent A sequence label.
.IP " 4." 4
B: parent B sequence label.
.IP " 5." 4
T: top parent sequence label (parent most similar to the query).
.IP " 6." 4
idQM: percentage of similarity between query (Q) and the model
.RS 4
.IP "(M)" 4
constructed as a part of parent A and a part of parent B.
.RE
.IP " 7." 4
idQA: percentage of similarity between query (Q) and parent A.
.IP " 8." 4
idQB: percentage of similarity between query (Q) and parent B.
.IP " 9." 4
idAB: percentage of similarity between parent A and parent B.
.IP "10." 4
idQT: percentage of similarity between query (Q) and top parent (T).
.IP "11." 4
LY: yes votes in the left part of the model.
.IP "12." 4
LN: no votes in the left part of the model.
.IP "13." 4
LA: abstain votes in the left part of the model.
.IP "14." 4
RY: yes votes in the right part of the model.
.IP "15." 4
RN: no votes in the right part of the model.
.IP "16." 4
RA: abstain votes in the right part of the model.
.IP "17." 4
div: divergence, defined as (idQM - idQT).
.IP "18." 4
YN: query is chimeric (Y), not chimeric (N), or borderline (?).
.RE
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--abskew\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Set the minimum abundance skew ratio between a chimera and its potential
parent.
The assumption is that chimeras appear later in the PCR amplification
process and are therefore less abundant than their parents.
Any positive value equal or greater than 1.0 can be used.
Default is 16.0, which means that the parents should be at least 16
times more abundant than the chimera.
.TP
\f[V]--dn\f[R] \f[I]strictly positive real number\f[R]
Set the pseudo-count prior on the number of `no' votes, corresponding to
the parameter \f[I]n\f[R] in the chimera scoring function.
Default value is 1.4.
Increasing \f[V]--dn\f[R] reduces the likelihood of tagging a sequence
as a chimera (fewer false positives, but also more false negatives).
.TP
\f[V]--sizein\f[R]
Use the abundance annotations present in sequence headers when reading
fasta or fastq file.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Entries without abundance annotations are silently assumed to be of
\f[V]size=1\f[R].
Always implied.
.TP
\f[V]--uchimeout5\f[R]
When using \f[V]--uchimeout\f[R], write chimera detection results using
a 17-field, tab-separated uchime-like format.
This drops the 5th field (top parent T) from the standard
\f[V]--uchimeout\f[R] format, for compatibility with usearch version 5
and earlier versions.
.TP
\f[V]--xn\f[R] \f[I]real number strictly greater than 1.0\f[R]
Set the weight of `no' votes, corresponding to the parameter
\f[I]beta\f[R] in the chimera scoring function.
Default value is 8.0.
Increasing \f[V]--xn\f[R] reduces the likelihood of tagging a sequence
as a chimera (less false positives, but also more false negatives).
Decreasing \f[V]--xn\f[R] reduces false negative, but increases false
positives.
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--alignwidth\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the width of the three-way alignments written with
\f[V]--uchimealns\f[R].
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_score\f[R]
Add the chimera score to the sequence headers in the fasta output files
for chimeras, non-chimeras, and borderline sequences, using the format
\f[V];uchime_denovo=float\f[R] (appended at the end of the header).
.TP
\f[V]--fasta_width\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the maximal width of sequences when writing fasta files.
Longer sequences are folded and written on several lines.
Default width is 80 nucleotides.
Set to zero (0) to suppress folding.
.TP
\f[V]--hardmask\f[R]
Replace masked nucleotides with Ns.
.TP
\f[V]--label_suffix\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the suffix \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers when writing fasta
or fastq files.
For example, with \f[V]--label_suffix \[dq];status=healthy\[dq]\f[R],
sequence header `>seq1' becomes `>seq1;status=healthy'.
.TP
\f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
Add a sequence length annotation (\f[V];length=integer\f[R]) to each
sequence header when writing fasta or fastq files.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
.TP
\f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] (50,000
nucleotides by default).
The value must not exceed 2,147,481,646 (\f[V]INT_MAX\f[R] minus 2,001).
.TP
\f[V]--minseqlength\f[R] \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R]
Discard sequences shorter than \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R] (1
nucleotide by default).
A value of 0 retains empty sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]
Retain whole sequence headers in output files.
By default, vsearch truncates sequence headers at first space or
tabulation.
This option suppresses truncation.
.TP
\f[V]--qmask\f[R] \f[I]none\f[R]|\f[I]dust\f[R]|\f[I]soft\f[R]
Mask regions in query sequences using the \f[I]dust\f[R] method or the
\f[I]soft\f[R] method, or \f[I]none\f[R] to suppress masking.
Values are case-insensitive, so \f[V]DUST\f[R], \f[V]Dust\f[R], and
\f[V]dust\f[R] are all accepted.
See \f[V]vsearch-fastx_mask(1)\f[R] for more details.
Warning, when using \f[I]soft\f[R] masking, search commands become case
sensitive: masking excludes masked regions from the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer
pre-filter that selects candidate targets (the pairwise alignment itself
always ignores case).
A query with no unmasked stretch of at least the word length samples no
\f[I]k\f[R]-mers and is therefore compared against every database
sequence.
The default is to mask using \f[I]dust\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Replace sequence headers with the prefix \f[I]string\f[R] and a ticker
(1, 2, 3, etc.).
For example, with \f[V]--relabel \[dq]cluster:\[dq]\f[R], the first
sequence header becomes `>cluster:1', the second sequence header becomes
`>cluster:2', and so on.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R]
Retain old sequence identifiers by including them at the end of the new
headers, after a space.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the MD5 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The MD5 digest is a 128-bit value (16 bytes), represented using a string
of 32 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]md5(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_self\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the sequence itself.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--relabel_sha1\f[R]
Replace each sequence header with the SHA1 digest derived from the
sequence itself.
The sequence is converted to upper case, and each `U' is replaced with a
`T' before computation of the digest.
The SHA1 digest is a 160-bit value (20 bytes), represented using a
string of 40 ASCII characters.
Each pair of characters encodes an hexadecimal value, ranging from
\f[V]x00\f[R] to \f[V]xff\f[R].
See \f[V]sha1(3)\f[R] for more details, and \f[V]--relabel_md5\f[R] for
an alternative hashing algorithm.
To retain annotations, use their corresponding options
(\f[V]--lengthout\f[R], \f[V]--eeout\f[R], and \f[V]--sizeout\f[R]).
Use \f[V]--relabel_keep\f[R] to also retain old sequence identifiers.
.TP
\f[V]--sample\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Add the given sample identifier \f[I]string\f[R] to sequence headers
when writing fasta or fastq files.
For instance, if \f[I]string\f[R] is `ABC', the text
\f[V];sample=ABC\f[R] will be added to the headers.
\f[I]string\f[R] is silently truncated at the first `;' or whitespace
character (space, tab, newline, carriage return, vertical tab or form
feed), so such characters should not be used in \f[I]string\f[R].
Other characters (alphabetical, numerical and punctuations) are
accepted.
.TP
\f[V]--sizeout\f[R]
Add abundance annotations to sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Add the pattern \f[V];size=integer\f[R].
If option \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is not used, abundance values are set to 1
for all entries.
If \f[V]--sizein\f[R] is used, existing abundance annotations are simply
reported to output files.
.TP
\f[V]--xee\f[R]
Strip expected error (ee) annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]ee=float[;]\f[R].
Expected error annotations are added by the synonymous options
\f[V]--fastq_eeout\f[R] and \f[V]--eeout\f[R] described in
\f[V]vsearch-fastx_filter(1)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--xlength\f[R]
Strip sequence length annotations from sequence headers when writing
fasta or fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]length=integer[;]\f[R].
Sequence length annotations are added by the \f[V]--lengthout\f[R]
option.
.TP
\f[V]--xsize\f[R]
Strip abundance annotations from sequence headers when writing fasta or
fastq files.
Search for the pattern \f[V][>\[at];]size=integer[;]\f[R].
Abundance annotations are added by the \f[V]--sizeout\f[R] option.
.SS pairwise alignment options
.PP
These options modify the parameters of the pairwise alignment model.
Modify with caution.
.TP
\f[V]--gapext\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Set penalties for a gap extension.
See \f[V]vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters(7)\f[R] for a complete
description of the gap penalty declaration system.
By default, the penalty is set to 2 for extending internal gaps and to 1
for extending terminal gaps, in both query and target sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Set penalties for a gap opening.
See \f[V]vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters(7)\f[R] for a complete
description of the gap penalty declaration system.
By default, the penalty is set to 20 for opening internal gaps and to 2
for opening terminal gaps, in both query and target sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--match\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Set the score assigned to a match (i.e.\ equivalent nucleotides) in
pairwise alignments.
The default value is 2.
Accepted values range from -32767 to +32767.
.TP
\f[V]--mismatch\f[R] \f[I]integer\f[R]
Set the score assigned to a mismatch (i.e.\ different nucleotides) in
pairwise alignments.
The default value is -4.
Accepted values range from -32767 to +32767.
.SS ignored options
.TP
\f[V]--mindiffs\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the minimum number of differences per segment.
Default value is 3.
Ignored by \f[V]--uchime3_denovo\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--mindiv\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Set the minimum divergence from the closest parent.
Default value is 0.8.
Ignored by \f[V]--uchime3_denovo\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--minh\f[R] \f[I]real\f[R]
Set the minimum chimera score (\f[I]h\f[R]).
Increasing this value tends to reduce the number of false positives and
to decrease sensitivity.
Default value is 0.28.
Accepted values are strictly greater than 0.0; values above 1.0 are
accepted but uncommon.
Ignored by \f[V]--uchime3_denovo\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive non-null integer\f[R]
Command is not multithreaded, option has no effect (a warning is printed
when more than one thread is requested).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Detect chimeras \f[I]de novo\f[R] using the UCHIME2 algorithm with a
strict abundance skew, and write non-chimeric sequences to a file:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --uchime3_denovo denoised.fasta \[rs]
    --sizein \[rs]
    --nonchimeras clean.fasta
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
A typical denoising workflow: dereplicate, denoise, then remove chimeras
(recommended approach after \f[V]--cluster_unoise\f[R]):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --derep_fulllength reads.fasta \[rs]
    --sizeout \[rs]
    --output derep.fasta

vsearch \[rs]
    --cluster_unoise derep.fasta \[rs]
    --sizein \[rs]
    --sizeout \[rs]
    --centroids denoised.fasta

vsearch \[rs]
    --uchime3_denovo denoised.fasta \[rs]
    --sizein \[rs]
    --nonchimeras amplicons.fasta
\f[R]
.fi
